High-level flow
Takes one TIL base number (currently hardcoded as 1603).
Looks up matching TIL PDF path in Databricks Volumes.
Downloads that PDF bytes.
Sends the PDF to GE Foundation Service PDF extraction API.
Parses API output into:
combined text content (text + image descriptions + unlabeled tables),
labeled table objects (only when table labels like “Table N:” are detected).
Saves 3 JSON files under a per-TIL results folder:
parsed text,
parsed labeled tables,
raw API response.
What each main function does
call_foundation_service:
builds multipart request with PDF + params (mode, process_mode, etc.),
calls the extraction endpoint,
returns JSON on HTTP 200, else prints error and returns empty dict.
parse_foundation_response:
loops page-by-page through response results,
collects text, table, image blocks,
detects labeled tables by regex from page text: Table <number>: ...,
sends labeled tables to structured table output,
pushes unlabeled tables and image descriptions into main text body.
read_til_1b_foundation:
orchestrates end-to-end run,
handles basic failure states (pdf_not_found, download fail, API fail),
writes timestamped output files in results/<til_base_num>/.
Output shape
You get a practical split:

Text JSON: full content for downstream LLM/profile extraction.
Tables JSON: only explicitly labeled tables with page, label, description, source, content.
Raw JSON: full Foundation response for debugging/replay.
Notable assumptions / risks
It only processes the first matched PDF (pdf_paths[0]), not all matches.
SSL verification is disabled (verify=False) and warnings are suppressed.
Proxy bypass is forced (proxies={"http": "", "https": ""}).
Table label detection currently uses one regex search on page text, so multiple labels on one page may not be fully captured.
Non-200 API handling is basic (no retry/backoff).


