What output looks like:

Matching layer:

Confirms requested TIL and matched PDF path.
Includes normalized key, base TIL number, revision, and match type.
Markdown version is a small human-readable table.
Raw extraction layer:

Page-wise structure under results with keys like 1, 2, 3.
Each page has typed blocks: text, table, image.
Table content is markdown-like text blobs; image content is OCR/vision description text.
This is the closest artifact to foundation service native output.
Normalized extracted document layer:

Flattened text content across pages into one long body.
Includes inline markers such as unlabeled table from page N and image description from page N.
Metadata includes text length and labeled table count.
For this sample: text_length is 24256, labeled_table_count is 0.
Markdown version is review-friendly and includes field table plus full extracted text.
Profile layer:

Structured semantic profile built from extracted text.
Includes fields like compliance category, outage trigger, scope of work, service recommendations, intervals, risk/failure consequences, reference docs, configuration dependencies, source snippets, confidence.
For this sample, extraction_confidence is 0.78, SBOM dependency is false, and parts_referenced is empty.
Markdown version is very readable for SME review; JSON version is downstream-consumable.
Observed quality characteristics in this 1603 sample:

Strengths:

Good capture of narrative content and planning logic.
Useful structured profile fields for applicability reasoning.
Both human-readable and machine-usable outputs are present.
Gaps/noise visible:

Some image descriptions are noisy/gibberish text.
Labeled table count in extracted_document is 0 even though raw extraction has table blocks; indicates strict label detection behavior in normalization.
Boilerplate legal text is repeated heavily across pages.